---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const comparativeCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · One medicine, many bodies', note: 'A vet works across species whose hearts, guts and metabolisms differ radically — comparative anatomy is the daily toolkit.' },
    { math: '2 · The ruminant\'s factory stomach', note: 'Cattle ferment grass in a four-chambered forestomach: microbes digest fibre, the host digests the microbes. A walking bioreactor.' },
    { math: '3 · Birds breathe one-way', note: 'Air flows through avian lungs in one direction via air sacs — more efficient than the mammalian in-and-out design, built for flight.' },
    { math: '4 · Parasites are species-specific', note: 'Heartworm in dogs, liver fluke in sheep, coccidia in poultry: parasitology is a zoo of host-specialised life cycles, each with its own control point.' },
    { math: '5 · Drugs must respect the differences', note: 'A dose safe in a dog can kill a cat (paracetamol); penicillin is useless against mycoplasma. Comparative pharmacology keeps patients alive.' },
  ],
};

const hrCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'hr', label: 'resting heart rate', min: 20, max: 350, step: 5, value: 60, unit: 'bpm' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'approx. body mass', expr: 'pow(2130/hr, 0.75)', unit: 'kg' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/veterinary-medicine/foundational/veterinary-biomedical-sciences">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Veterinary medicine is clinical medicine without a single patient species. Its foundation is
    comparative: the same organ systems, radically different engineering. The cow runs a microbial
    fermentation vat, the bird a one-way lung, the cat a liver that cannot process paracetamol.
    Master the differences and the discipline opens; ignore them and the patient dies.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="comparative physiology: the same systems, different designs" widget="equationStep" config={comparativeCfg} />

  <h2>Allometry: size predicts biology</h2>
  <p>
    Across mammals, heart rate scales with body mass to roughly the −¼ power: a 700 kg cow beats
    about 50 times a minute, a 3 kg cat about 180. The slider inverts the classic allometric
    equation heart rate ≈ 2130 × mass^(−0.75) — drag the heart rate and estimate the animal on the
    table. Small animals live fast; large ones live slow.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="heart rate → estimated body mass (allometry)" widget="sliderLab" config={hrCfg} />

  <h2>Parasitology is half the job</h2>
  <p>
    From fleas to liver fluke, parasites drive more routine presentations than any other cause, and
    each life cycle hides a control point: interrupt the intermediate host, the grazing window, or
    the environmental stage. Rational deworming — timed to the cycle, tested with faecal counts —
    has replaced the calendar-dosing era, largely because resistance taught the profession humility.
  </p>

  <h2>One Health: the veterinary door into human health</h2>
  <p>
    Most emerging human infections are zoonotic — they come from animals. Veterinary medicine sits
    on that border: surveillance in livestock and wildlife, food safety from farm to fork, and the
    shared antimicrobial-resistance fight. The vet is public health with a stethoscope.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NAVLE blueprint (ICVA); Cunningham, <em>Textbook of Veterinary Physiology</em>; Schmidt-Nielsen, <em>Animal Physiology</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
